# Line endings are content here, so git is told rather than asked.
#
# Without a rule, core.autocrlf decides, and it is a per-machine setting.
# In the Application Requirement Ontology, which this setup is carried
# from, a Windows checkout produced CRLF and a Linux one LF, and the two
# disagreed about the contents of every multi-line Turtle literal --
# because a CRLF inside a literal is part of that literal's value, not
# formatting around it.
#
# This repository has the same exposure, and one more: the v0.1 draft
# package under drafts/ carries its own SHA256SUMS.txt, and
# docs/ARTIFACT-INVENTORY.md records a digest for every imported file.
# Those digests were taken over LF bytes. A checkout that rewrote them as
# CRLF would fail its own checksums while every file read the same.
#
# The rule is repository-wide rather than limited to *.ttl: any digest
# recorded over a file git is free to re-encode is not a digest.
#
# Applied before the corpus grew, which is the cheap moment.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Redundant under the rule above, and kept anyway: the corpus is the
# reason the rule exists, and a future narrowing of `*` should not
# silently take the corpus with it.
*.ttl text eol=lf
*.tsv text eol=lf

# Declared binary so content detection is not what stands between these
# and a corrupting newline translation.
*.pdf -text
*.zip -text
*.png -text
*.xlsx -text
.DS_Store -text
